# Keep the dependency lockfiles LF on every platform so the CI sync-check (`uv export` +
# `git diff --exit-code`) can't be tripped by Windows CRLF drift (DEP-1).
uv.lock text eol=lf
requirements.lock text eol=lf
docker/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
# constraints.lock was MISSING here (`git check-attr text -- constraints.lock` -> unspecified), so under
# core.autocrlf=true it checks out CRLF — precisely the drift this stanza exists to prevent. Harmless so
# far only because git's clean filter normalizes before `git diff`, which is also why export sync must be
# verified with `git diff` and never a raw `diff` (the latter reports a phantom whole-file difference).
constraints.lock text eol=lf
# The PEP 735 CI-toolchain exports (ADR 0034 §3) — same DEP-1 diff gate, same LF requirement.
ci/locks/*.lock text eol=lf
.mefor-hooks/** text eol=lf
# Shell scripts are executed on the Linux CI runners; force LF so a contributor's
# core.autocrlf=true can never commit CRLF (which breaks `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r`).
*.sh text eol=lf

# The vendored CLA Action's compiled bundle (BACKLOG #1364): its SHA256 was checked against the
# exact upstream commit and that checksum is recorded as evidence in the ledger entry. `-text`
# disables ALL line-ending conversion so the stored and checked-out bytes never drift from what
# was verified -- `eol=lf` was tried first and silently re-encoded ~1,300 bytes, which would have
# committed a file that no longer matches its own recorded checksum.
.github/actions/**/dist/** -text

# The offline password-screening corpus and its notice (BACKLOG #1433). The corpus SHA-256 is
# recorded in the notice's generated block and recomputed by `scripts/security/build_password_corpus.py`,
# so the checked-out bytes must not depend on the platform.
# THE ATTRIBUTE WAS MISSING ENTIRELY BEFORE #1433 -- `git check-attr text` reported `unspecified`, so
# under `core.autocrlf=true` the file checked out CRLF while the blob held LF: two SHA-256 values for
# one committed file (fd9786f1 vs 136e7bcf). Adding a pin, not changing one.
# `eol=lf` RATHER THAN `-text`, and the difference is load-bearing for a file a generator rewrites.
# `-text` disables the clean filter, so it FREEZES the current bytes and does nothing about the next
# write: a later CRLF edit lands CRLF in the blob permanently. `eol=lf` NORMALIZES on the way in, so
# the invariant the recorded digest depends on keeps holding. The `-text` stanza above is for the CLA
# bundle, whose blob genuinely CONTAINS CRLF; this blob holds none, so `eol=lf` re-encodes nothing
# here. `text=auto` (not bare `text`) defers to git's binary detection.
# Changing an attribute does not rewrite an existing working tree: an older checkout keeps its CRLF
# copy until `git checkout -- <path>`. The generator normalizes CRLF to LF before digesting so such a
# checkout still verifies rather than failing with a hex mismatch that names nothing.
messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.txt text=auto eol=lf
messagefoundry/auth/data/common_passwords.NOTICE text=auto eol=lf
